The best approach for `File.*` and `Directory.*` is the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package — it's battle-tested and already has a mock filesystem for tests.

## Setup

**1. Install packages:**
```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**2. Register in DI** (Program.cs / Startup.cs):
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
public class ConfigLoader(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool ConfigDirExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});

var loader = new ConfigLoader(mockFs);

// File.ReadAllText
Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", loader.Load("/config.json"));

// File.WriteAllText
loader.Save("/out.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/out.txt"));

// Directory.Exists
Assert.False(loader.ConfigDirExists("/missing"));
```

## Why this over a custom wrapper?

| | Custom wrapper | System.IO.Abstractions |
|---|---|---|
| **Coverage** | Only what you wrap | Full `File`, `Directory`, `Path`, `Stream` surface |
| **Maintenance** | You own it | Community-maintained, well-tested |
| **Test helpers** | Build your own mock | `MockFileSystem` included |

The `IFileSystem` interface mirrors the real `System.IO` API, so the migration is mostly mechanical — replace `File.` with `fileSystem.File.` and `Directory.` with `fileSystem.Directory.`.

> **Tip:** Once the package is installed, you can use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill to bulk-replace all call sites automatically.
